GList.hh: the move constructor is *declared* without `noexcept` but *defined*
with it. Match the declaration to the definition.

Upstream only ever compiles with `-std=c++11 ... -fno-exceptions`, where clang
skips the exception-specification equivalence check, so the mismatch never
surfaced. Consumers on OHOS default to `-std=c++17`, where `noexcept` is part
of the function type and the mismatch is a hard error:

    GList.hh:375:34: error: exception specification in declaration does not
    match previous declaration
    template <class OBJ> GList<OBJ>::GList(GList<OBJ>&& other) noexcept:
    GList.hh:101:5: note: previous declaration is here
        GList(GList<OBJ>&& list); //move constructor

Fixing the declaration (rather than dropping `noexcept` from the definition)
preserves the author's explicit intent, so the move constructor stays
non-throwing and `std::vector` keeps using it for reallocation.

--- a/GList.hh
+++ b/GList.hh
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@
     GList(bool sorted, bool free_elements=true, bool beUnique=false);
     GList(int init_capacity, bool sorted, bool free_elements=true, bool beUnique=false);
     GList(const GList<OBJ>& list); //copy constructor
-    GList(GList<OBJ>&& list); //move constructor
+    GList(GList<OBJ>&& list) noexcept; //move constructor
     GList(GList<OBJ>* list); //kind of a copy constructor
     GList<OBJ>& operator=(GList<OBJ>& list); //copy operator
     GList<OBJ>& operator=(GList<OBJ>&& list); //move operator
